{"r":13,"c":[[54,"There are two categories of interface to the OPT3001: electrical and optical."]]}
{"r":14,"c":[[54,"8.1.1"],[85,"Electrical Interface"]]}
{"r":15,"c":[[54,"The electrical interface is quite simple, as illustrated in Figure 29. Connect the OPT3001 I2C SDA and SCL pins"]]}
{"r":16,"c":[[54,"to the same pins of an applications processor, microcontroller, or other digital processor. If that digital processor"]]}
{"r":17,"c":[[54,"requires an interrupt resulting from an event of interest from the OPT3001, then connect the INT pin to either an"]]}
{"r":18,"c":[[54,"interrupt or general-purpose I/O pin of the processor. There are multiple uses for this interrupt, including signaling"]]}
{"r":19,"c":[[54,"the system to wake up from low-power mode, processing other tasks while waiting for an ambient light event of"]]}
{"r":20,"c":[[54,"interest, or alerting the processor that a sample is ready to be read. Connect pullup resistors between a power"]]}
{"r":21,"c":[[54,"supply appropriate for digital communication and the SDA and SCL pins (because they have open-drain output"]]}
{"r":22,"c":[[54,"structures). If the INT pin is used, connect a pullup resistor to the INT pin. A typical value for these pullup"]]}
{"r":23,"c":[[54,"resistors is 10 kΩ. The resistor choice can be optimized in conjunction to the bus capacitance to balance the"]]}

